00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.060
 Welcome to the show, guys.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.700
 Well, let's start with, how did you become farmers?

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.770
 It was such a...

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:50.930
 Okay, it was a really convoluted journey that kind of was all coming together right about the time I met Molly.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:58.430
 I was in school for sustainable design in Burlington, Vermont, and it was a self-directed course of study.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:03.270
 And I was deciding whether I wanted to be an architect, go to architecture school, or go to landscape architecture school.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:04.940
 Had been really...

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:15.920
 Had my mind blown open by my permaculture class I had recently done, and was just really feeling engaged with the design part of, you know, the future world that I was wanting to build and make a better place.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.800
 And I went to this place called Yes Tomorrow, which is a design-build school in Warren, Vermont, that you should all check out.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.460
 It's a really cool, empowering place, teaching lots of amazing skills.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:31.340
 And I was an intern there and ran their gardens as part of my internship.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:42.900
 And just the design component, the eating component, planning something, making something, finishing it at the end of the season, putting it to bed, was just all really appealing.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:53.120
 And I met Molly there and decided to come to California, pursue another design job, and think about what the hell to do.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:04:02.840
 And we decided to do a farm apprenticeship together because she had been interested in doing the program at UCSC and was still, I think, toying with that idea when we met.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:03.990
 Yeah.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.310
 And we kind of farmed our way north from there.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.020
 Yeah, my intention, I just want to ask a follow-up question, too, Zach.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.320
 So going from, like, a garden scale to a farm scale is a pretty big leap.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.300
 It is a pretty big leap.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:27.410
 It was, well, because a garden's kind of really easy to keep and isn't, you know, it was like I did it part-time and that wasn't going to pay me.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:34.650
 And I was really having this mental shift of, like, do I want to go learn to draw cab lines and work indoors or whatever.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.850
 Landscape architecture was feeling more appealing, systems design, that kind of stuff.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.430
 And I didn't want to do any more school, really.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:48.130
 And I wanted to get to work and work outside and work with my body and work with my mind.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:55.790
 And I knew kind of from the get-go when we started that if it was a route to take, it needed to pay us or me.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:04.550
 It wasn't something I could just go to with, like, the ethic of right livelihood that was kind of driving any future career decisions at that point.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.570
 So it just seemed, yeah, it just was kind of an obvious choice.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:11.180
 And how about you, Molly?

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.440
 How did you become a farmer?

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:23.500
 Well, as Zach mentioned, when he first came to California, I was getting set to do a farm apprenticeship with the goal of working in an educational setting.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:31.920
 I've always been interested in sort of alternative, particularly residential schools, like they have on the East Coast, lots of different farm schools.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:38.940
 And so it was my hope to sort of be a staff member running a farm program at a school.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.350
 That was sort of my vague goal.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:42.930
 So that was my reason for going into it.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:48.670
 And when Zach came to California, we decided to do an internship together.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:55.560
 So we went up to Washington State and worked at Persephone Farm with Rebecca Slattery, which was really great.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.140
 And that's how we got started.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.300
 She had a really wonderful business model at the kind of scale we could imagine.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.170
 She has a 60-member CSA, markets, and she has flowers.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:13.530
 So it was interesting for me because I had intended to do an internship at CASFIS, which is an educational program.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.760
 So it sort of would have kept that going for me.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:23.700
 Seeing a market scale farm that's actually in the business side had a big impact.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.580
 And at that time, though, I said, this is really great, but this isn't really what I want to do.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.160
 And I was like, all right, this could work.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:41.770
 Yeah, I could see how we could do this and, you know, did and still do dream and scheme about segueing educational components in and things like that.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:50.850
 But it's kind of the reality of doing it has run up on us really fast in a way that's like, okay, we can do all that stuff in due time.

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:53.410
 But we also have to, like, we have a one-year-old daughter now.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.510
 We've got to think about the future.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:56.830
 We've got to pay the bills.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:02.730
 We've got to buy a flail mower so I don't have to bend over and rip out crops so many times and hurt my back over the next 30 years.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.170
 You know, it's just, it's changed the thought process.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:06.430
 Yeah.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.930
 Can you describe your farm?

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:09.970
 Yeah.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.510
 Our current farm?

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:11.870
 Yeah.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.540
 Yeah, it's a 24-acre property.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.960
 We farm, I guess, just over two acres now of mixed vegetables.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.840
 Yeah, something like that, two and a half acres, two and a half acres.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:25.920
 We started out integrating animals as much as possible.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.880
 We're still doing that to a smaller extent.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:28.520
 To a smaller degree.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:34.180
 We do mostly vegetables, but we're adding in more and more flowers.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.940
 We have a 50-member CSA.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.070
 We're going up to 65 this year.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.700
 We sell at two markets.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:41.980
 Some grocery stores.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:42.360
 Yeah.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.630
 So pretty diverse in that way.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:51.080
 We have had more animals in the past, and now we have just sheep.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:51.830
 Now we have sheep.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:52.150
 And pigs.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:59.750
 You worked for Rebecca as interns at Persephone Farm in Indianola, right?

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:00.390
 In Washington.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:06.780
 And then after that, you went to go look for a place to have a farm, or how did that?

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:10.600
 That was kind of paralleling just our journey, right?

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.400
 Yeah, we were sort of vaguely looking.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:13.660
 Vaguely thinking of doing that.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:15.280
 Yeah.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:21.760
 We went to our friend, Bree Eagle, that we went to UCSC with, lives on Salt Spring Island at her own family farm there.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:23.690
 Which is where your farm is on Salt Spring Island.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:24.690
 Yes, on Salt Spring Island.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:25.110
 Exactly.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:28.990
 So we went and worked with her for a season, and then went back for another season.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.480
 And one thing led to another, and now we're still there on our own farm.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:34.570
 But we weren't looking.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:39.250
 We weren't looking to move to Canada and to settle on a small island and have a farm there.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.170
 That just kind of happened.

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:53.230
 We were vaguely looking from the Bay Area north to Vancouver Island as we were spending our non-farm apprentice season back here in Sonoma County and exploring the West Coast.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:58.660
 Well, when I think of your farm, I think of, like, this coming together of a lot of different visions, right?

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:04.230
 So this is, we had, Devin and I went to your farm this summer.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.350
 It's like, it's totally beautiful, right?

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:13.850
 It's this incredible piece of land that is on this amazing island, like, surrounded by orcas.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:18.930
 And there's a pond, and there's, you know, there's flowers, and there's this huge variety of vegetables.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.990
 And you have interns, and your community is pretty involved.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.570
 There's people there a lot.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:26.030
 It's a really vibrant situation.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.480
 You also have a cottage that people are coming to stay at.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:30.140
 No more.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:31.000
 No more.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:31.500
 No more.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:38.280
 We made a decision this year to not, that the farm was at a place financially where we think we can afford to not do that.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:39.800
 And it's going to feel really good.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:41.780
 And we've had some, yeah, really great guests.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:50.410
 But also it's a huge, it can be a huge headache and distraction from, like, what our actual mission is, which is to, you know, grow food for our community.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.690
 Yeah, well, so let's talk about that vision.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:10:06.180
 I think that's, like, what really stood out to me about our visit this summer was thinking about, like, what that means to commit to a place and work on it over time with a set of changing conditions, right?

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.360
 You were injured this year.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.560
 You have Zach, you know, you both have a new daughter who's a year old.

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:16.700
 You are bringing more people on to be involved in different capacities.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.850
 Where do you hope it goes?

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:21.430
 What do you see?

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:23.470
 It's that time of year to talk about it.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.320
 Well, we love to keep just adding more and more things.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:30.000
 That's one of our passions.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.330
 Sometimes it feels like we just, like, let the horse run.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:35.590
 I don't know if that's a thing or not.

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.290
 But, like, it's just running away from us and we're holding on.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:42.840
 And steering in a positive direction so far has worked.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.760
 But I think where we want it to go, there's so many.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:48.640
 We get really distracted.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:57.600
 We kind of want a school and a restaurant and a concert hall and wedding venue and to have 100-member CSA and processed food.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.760
 You know, we want to do everything and engage our community in that process.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:02.100
 Yeah.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.940
 We do really love having the farm be a vibrant place.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.900
 That's important to our community.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:09.760
 There's a huge amount of tourism on Salt Springs.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.070
 So the markets there are great but pretty tourist-driven to some extent.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.950
 So increasing our CSA is something that is a sure goal for us.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:18.330
 Yeah.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:19.630
 We've added a flower CSA.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.300
 All of our members pick up on the farm.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:23.040
 So they're very involved.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:24.170
 We love that.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:28.030
 We get a lot of feedback from them and take what they say pretty seriously.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.530
 And they love feeling like they're such a part of it.

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:34.750
 And we've gotten to, you know, know a lot of our members really well.

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:41.710
 We have one group of members that every time Google Maps updates the farm because they've been with us since the beginning.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:48.390
 And every time it's updated on Google Earth, they print us this huge, you know, wall-sized printout.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.750
 They're like, look, here's the new field.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.710
 And, oh, look, there's when the greenhouse went up.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.270
 And, you know, we've got this really cool timeline that way.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.290
 And, you know, now we have people watching Larkin grow up.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:05.280
 And it just feels really good to have people going on this journey with us, you know, because it can be terrifying sometimes, right?

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:05.640
 Yeah.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:06.440
 That's true.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:06.780
 Yeah.

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.040
 So we're definitely going to keep moving in that direction.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:18.590
 And we are working on a possible new project, adding a barn, like an events barn that will have a multi-use capacity.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:24.790
 Yeah, a multi-use facility with the commercial kitchen and doing concerts and weddings and things like that.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:24.910
 Yeah.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:29.880
 And that feels really exciting because we like the diversity in our – and that feels sort of stressful.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:35.780
 It feels like a great place for collaboration and continuing to bring other people into, you know.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.760
 Farms have such a huge –

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.490
 potential to involve people.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:40.070
 Yes.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:50.220
 And, you know, whether it's with work or, you know, art or whatever, there's just – they can just take human input like, you know, no other – nothing else I can really think about.

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:53.580
 And that just is always constantly inspiring.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.380
 And I think we're just going to kind of keep letting it run.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:58.200
 Yeah.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:03.060
 And in the background, I'm always still like, well, this is not my real job.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.320
 I'm starting a farm school.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.230
 Once we build this big farm, we can fill it with, you know, programs like a farm school.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:14.170
 And we can start doing it in sessions involving other schools that already have things running.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:23.430
 And we don't have to be school directors also because we tend to stretch ourselves so thin thinking, like, I could be the director of a school or own a restaurant and run the farm.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:23.750
 No.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:24.630
 It's true.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:30.950
 But when I'm, you know, washing salad for the thousandth hour, I'm often like, oh, well, that's fine.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:34.720
 It's not part of the plan to, you know, build up this –

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.170
 Build up this system that supports all these other people.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:38.030
 Yeah, exactly.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:38.650
 Naive or not.

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:38.990
 I don't know.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:39.680
 Yeah.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:49.060
 When I hear you both talk about this, I hear that a farm is both just a farm and it's also never just a farm, right?

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:55.750
 So, Zach, you kind of came from this place of thinking about systems and design that way.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:14:12.120
 And, you know, Molly, like, a lot of what happened in your life before being a farmer was that you were, like, really fascinated with the natural world and really liked inviting people to be part of that in so many different ways.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.800
 And so, what's a farm?

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:18.890
 Right.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:20.580
 That's – it's funny.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:31.680
 That is a particularly – a question I, like, rant or ask about a lot because on Salt Spring, there's probably a hundred properties with signs that say farm.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:32.660
 Let's just call it that.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:36.400
 And, you know, but that's – and so that's my, like, I'm a grumpy farmer side.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:43.700
 Like, farmers make money growing food to feed their community or – and then there's the whole, like, what is – like, what else does it produce?

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:45.370
 And, like, yeah, I don't know.

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:47.110
 I was getting tirage.

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:48.090
 I love this question.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.290
 Yeah, it really is.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:49.570
 What is a farm?

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.390
 What is a farm?

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:53.930
 I mean, it's like – what I like about it, it's a big project.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:56.880
 I love projects, things that involve a lot of people.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:04.590
 But then there's times when I'm, like, oh, farming is the worst invention ever, you know?

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.850
 You should be hunting these deer and blazing about by the lake.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:09.170
 Yeah.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.470
 Like, it does take so much input of different ways.

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:16.590
 And we're trying to close our system, but we're always paying attention to that while we're having to bring in from outside.

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:20.380
 And, yeah, what is a farm?

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:21.160
 Well, I mean, I –

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:21.880
 I think that's for a while.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:31.170
 It's like – my grumpy farmer definition is a farm is a place where people work the land to produce, you know, food for their community.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.570
 And, like, that's – but then they – yeah.

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:41.810
 I know other people will have different definitions to that, but I think in its essence, that's, like – I don't know.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:50.860
 Well, I think one way a farm could be thought about is that – is, like, in a conceptual art framework, right?

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.400
 So, like, food is your medium.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:53.700
 You produce it.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:57.740
 But it happens in all these different ways, and that changes over time, right?

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:05.660
 So there's this conceptual project of one season being about exploring how animals interact with the land.

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:11.080
 Or one season of being, like, what's the experiment when Zach only has one leg to use?

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:12.200
 Or whatever.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.550
 And it's always imperfect.

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:19.210
 And there's all these – I mean, there's endless challenges about it.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:23.050
 But that food is kind of the thing that runs through with that, right?

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:30.400
 Our friends – our friend Sue always says community is the message, food is the medium.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.420
 Which, yeah, is pretty resonant.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:35.770
 So what do you grow?

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.550
 We grow all the things, basically.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.810
 We just decided today we don't grow rutabaga.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:43.230
 We don't grow rutabaga, yeah.

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:44.310
 Besides that.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:49.030
 You know, we grow radishes, and we grow nine kinds of radishes or whatever.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.950
 We grow winter squash, and we grow 12 kinds of winter squash.

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:56.450
 And, I mean, we grow every – all the staple vegetables you can think of.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:04.310
 And then every year, and maybe more than once a year, we try to do, like, fun things that, you know, are –

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:11.200
 like, next year I'm growing these black French radishes that look like daikon that I'm really excited about.

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.300
 I'm so excited about them.

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:13.640
 I love growing radishes so much.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:14.580
 That's why I sit with it.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:15.140
 I don't know why.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:17.600
 I don't even love to eat them that much, but they're my favorite crop to grow.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:19.940
 We grow berries.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:21.180
 We grow flowers.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.280
 Yeah, lots of flowers, you know, probably.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:34.670
 A hundred varieties, 80 varieties or more of flowers, which in itself is a whole huge separate diversity thing that's really awesome.

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:40.170
 Yeah, you know, 12 types of tomatoes, five types – three types of zucchini.

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:45.690
 All the stuff you can think of in general, annual mixed vegetables, tayberries, raspberries, strawberries.

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:48.800
 And we just planted an orchard this year.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.520
 There's an old orchard on the farm.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:53.380
 We planted a little – an orchard for Larkins.

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:55.430
 I keep saying it's Larkins orchard.

00:17:55.950 --> 00:18:11.140
 Of 30 heritage apple trees that we grafted six years ago when we first arrived at the farm from a collection of 350 varieties of old English cider apples that we – from the farm we worked on before at Bright Farm.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:19.460
 We get to try all their best apples over the two years and then take scion wood and, you know, now those trees are, you know, eight feet tall and branched.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:22.560
 And some of them even have little fruits on them their first year to stress.

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:27.280
 But, you know, next year it's – it'll branch out nicely and it's great.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:30.760
 I think we're going to plant figs next year.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:31.480
 And some nuts.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:32.120
 Some nuts.

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:36.230
 We're part of an experiment – experiment.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:37.690
 Is that the right word?

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:40.100
 Yeah, I think it is actually.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:46.170
 With BC Ministry of Ag right now doing key lining research in our area.

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:55.930
 And that's a topic that I don't know if I should talk – it's basically slowing the flow of water out of your landscape is the kind of base thing.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.530
 But it takes – on all slopes there's a spot where there's a key line.

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:03.400
 Water will start to penetrate there as it's coming down the slope, kind of where it levels out.

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:06.180
 Our property has a valley in the middle of it.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:08.740
 I'm using my hands here for the benefit of Devin and Chelsea.

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:13.280
 But, you know, maybe everybody else can just stay with the words.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:19.630
 And the key line on our farm is where the two hills that are on the opposite sides of that valley meet.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.660
 So we have gone with a really deep chisel plow.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:23.600
 It's called a yeoman's plow.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.680
 It's from Australia that this grant funded the purchase of for –

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:30.770
 and the people found us in our property to work with.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:39.770
 And you lay it out – lay out deep plow lines on the contour – on contour across your contour.

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:42.890
 So essentially water moves from wet to dry.

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:46.370
 It's really – I'm not really good at explaining this.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:47.170
 But look it up.

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:50.710
 It's a very cool and potentially very useful strategy.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:20:02.170
 I mean, particularly for here in California, but hopefully for us as well, to keep our pastures green later, establish rows for nut trees, and, you know, keep water on things without us irrigating them.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:02.610
 Yeah.

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:03.270
 Alley cropping.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:07.830
 There's a guy named – oh, I'll blank it out.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.430
 He's from Mark Shepard.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:17.310
 And you should look up his farm if you want a really more intensive explanation of what key lining is all about.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:17.980
 Great.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:21.180
 We'll make sure to include a link for that so people can check it out.

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:30.950
 You talk a lot about your piece of land kind of like it's this third person in this collaboration.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.490
 And how do you – I mean, I think that's pretty common.

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:41.200
 I think farmers who work really closely with somewhere, you really intimately know a place, right?

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:49.080
 So how do you see yourselves as stewards of the land or not or, you know, what does that mean in the way that you decide to work there?

00:20:49.080 --> 00:21:02.130
 It's an interesting question because it's a 24-acre property, as I mentioned, and I think I would call it the most hesitant stewards of that.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:16.020
 There's a lot of infrastructure to keep up there that was already there, falling down fences and sheds and a long driveway and all these things that I remember at first we were like,

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:21.420
 Oh, gosh, it would be great if we just rent two acres and that's what we would be in charge of.

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.190
 Yeah, then we would have our – yeah.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:28.390
 Yeah, but over time, I think we have come to see ourselves that way.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:31.250
 I love all the parts of the farm.

00:21:31.370 --> 00:21:32.750
 Some of it's forested.

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:34.290
 We have some nice wetland zones.

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.570
 And so that's a really – it's really nice to be there.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:41.890
 And it's all of the – our farm's on eight – you called it a pond.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:43.150
 I mean, I'm not going to take offense.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:44.190
 It's called Bullock Lake.

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:50.790
 But it's got 800 feet at the bottom of frontage, at the bottom of – you know, we're essentially half the watershed for it.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:57.980
 So our practices would really have great effects on the lake if they were not, you know, up to par.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:08.540
 And when I go down there and there's this really nice tree at the bottom of the valley that's just this huge maple that is, you know, the middle busted out 50 years ago.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:19.510
 So it's all branch and you can sit in it and look out over the wetland and know that, like, the water that comes down our hills from our gardens, by the time it gets there, it's just clean water going down into the lake.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:34.870
 And that, you know, we're doing something to, like, feed our community and keep this wetland ecosystem intact for benefit of the lake, benefit of our, you know, children, of our friends, of our – it just – yeah, it's just a really good feeling.

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:37.690
 We're starting to make some of the bigger changes.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:38.190
 Yeah.

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:44.130
 We've been there five years now and we just dug an irrigation pond that's about to –

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:50.000
 Maybe we're going to overflow, but, you know, a lot of rain's happening and overflow's not quite ready yet.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:50.640
 Yeah.

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:55.530
 But – and we're building this barn, so that'll be in the forested section.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:23:00.360
 So we'll be taking out trees and that's a whole new interesting feeling for me.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:06.820
 Yeah, we hadn't really seriously altered the landscape in our, you know, soil management practices.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:08.020
 We're organic.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:10.080
 We're low tillage with small equipment.

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:11.860
 We grow tons of cover crops.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:18.070
 We've always taken that part of the stewardship very seriously, but at the same time, that's part of the, like, what is a farm?

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:29.040
 And this is the part of the farm that is, like, putting its little money-making box that, like, soil health and stewarding soil health just is – aside from being the right thing to do, without it, our business doesn't work.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:30.580
 So there's that part.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.880
 But now we're like, hey, those are big cedars.

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:37.480
 And those have to come down if we're going to build this other part of it.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:47.560
 So then, like, you know, we're really getting – we get an excavator in to do anything, and you're like, oh, we really need you to make these little pads so runoff doesn't just go to the storm drains.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:52.000
 And there's those kind of major land-altering decisions that are a little bit scary.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:52.480
 Yeah.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:54.920
 And we feel a strong sense of stewardship.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:58.400
 So it makes us sometimes really reluctant to make these major decisions.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:02.100
 And the woodland part of our farm, it's about seven acres.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:07.380
 It's full of English ivy and periwinkle that have been there for decades.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:12.600
 And, you know, I feel that responsibility when I go through there.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.160
 Oh, we need to get some goats in there and get that back under control and plant some salal.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:18.000
 But we just never get to it.

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:19.860
 So it kind of – it's interesting.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:21.600
 It goes into what you're talking about, what is a farm.

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:25.820
 And we kind of face that a bit with some of the – with interns in the past.

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:33.260
 Because a farm is so much more than your money-making crops and your rope section.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:42.240
 So that's just been interesting, trying to figure out for our interns, how do we give them the complete experience of storing this land and being charged with the farm?

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:50.110
 Because, like, the firewood that we do, you know, it's like captured sunshine, stored as carbon, burned again, keeping the farmers warm and alive.

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:53.070
 Like, it's still part of the same cycle.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:57.010
 And so there's always the, like, okay, this is the work that has to be done right now.

00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:59.770
 I know that's not bunching carrots and you want to learn how to grow – you know?

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:02.710
 So we try to, like, not have interns do those kind of jobs.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:04.370
 But still, that's part of the farm.

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:06.350
 And it's like the farmers are part of the farm.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:08.690
 And we're part of that whole dynamic ecosystem.

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:10.630
 And that's so really – yeah.

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:12.370
 Yeah, that's been an interesting balance to try to find.

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:15.650
 And sometimes it works and sometimes it's a bit harder.

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:16.150
 Yeah.

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:20.720
 What do you want the interns to leave feeling like a farm is?

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:22.530
 That's a good question.

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:26.830
 You got any thoughts on that?

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:30.880
 I'm really finding that a lot of my thoughts are visual.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:36.890
 Like, when I picture the farm, I really see it from above and sort of the humming network that it is.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:37.990
 You kind of do, too.

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:47.340
 I want them to leave with a sense that a farm is sort of – it's a place where it engages your whole self.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:50.190
 And to me, that's one of the most important things about it.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.570
 Yes, it's a place we grow food and it's how we make our living.

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:55.120
 And hopefully it can be a place where people learn.

00:25:57.100 --> 00:26:01.980
 There's so many aspects and it isn't perfect.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:15.070
 I want them to leave realizing that farming is, like, dirty and hard and beautiful and engages your full self and is not for everybody.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:22.760
 And that, you know, if one out of every 10 apprentices we get stays as a farmer and the other 10 are like,

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:28.600
 hey, it makes sense for me to pay four bucks for a bunch of carrots because I know how hard that works or was,

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:36.520
 then we're – or we, you know, pay – or train someone that engages with the food system in any other meaningful way as an advocate for small farms,

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:45.540
 then, yeah, I just want them to take away how important it is and, you know, how – yeah, just how important it is that we're all involved in it.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:54.500
 I think when I left Rebecca's, the most important thing that I took away, besides we learned so much when we model a lot of what we do off of her farm.

00:26:54.820 --> 00:27:02.030
 But the most important thing that still has me farming today is I left really understanding that it's beautiful.

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:02.950
 It's really beautiful.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:05.050
 And she really maintained that for us.

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:08.940
 All the different parts of it fitting together.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.290
 And sometimes it's really horrible.

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:15.450
 And we had horrible days there where I was like, this is the worst job I've ever had.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:20.650
 It's the first time it ever was like practically snowing on my birthday in June as a Southerner.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:22.790
 That was like really hard to cope with.

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:24.210
 I think I cried that day.

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:27.020
 It's feeling in the Northwest farming.

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:32.960
 So, yeah, there's days you're slugging through the mud and it's not so great, but it's beautiful.

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:35.200
 It's still part of this larger thing.

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:38.370
 And that was really important for me and it still is.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:42.080
 And I try to impart that to our interns as best I can.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:49.370
 And that there's other ways of, yeah, valuing even like I keep going to the economics thing,

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:53.350
 but like there's other ways to other things that get added into that, that, you know,

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:57.280
 we get to account for that other professions might not like that.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:28:01.700
 I eat the best watermelon every year, like, you know, and there's like those kind of things.

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:10.230
 And I want them to take away that like they should just really enjoy the ritualistic decadence of like the cycle of the seasons.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:17.900
 And hopefully the ones that want to farm have like taken away enough nuts and bolts skills to do a second year apprenticeship.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:24.240
 Because I mean, I think it's, yeah, or, or strike out on their own and learn some lessons.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:25.560
 That is like our concrete goal.

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:27.120
 We do say we want people to feel confident.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:30.600
 They could start a small scale operation that they could replicate our model.

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:30.760
 Yeah.

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:34.380
 Because we include them in every aspect of what we do down to the financial side.

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:35.760
 Down to account the money after the market.

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:41.320
 You know, telling them, you know, we're about to send all of our seed order in for this year.

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:42.280
 And then those will go right.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:44.200
 All those spreadsheets will go on to the apprentices.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:51.510
 So they see like, oh, here's what went into like deciding how many squash seeds do you need to grow for a, you know, 60 member CSA.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:52.970
 And I can see all the planning stuff.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:57.690
 And that is the, those are the pieces that have been, we've just figured that stuff out as we go.

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.070
 It's really, that's the hard, hard part.

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:06.240
 Well, when you both talk about this, it sounds like this has been kind of a journey to right livelihood.

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:07.060
 Right.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:08.940
 You, you're both college educated.

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:17.370
 You had a lot of choices about what you could do and you decided to like start a family farm and that's what you do.

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:36.410
 So, and I think that's really interesting to think about right livelihood as like, you know, that watermelons in the summer are part of like your benefits package or whatever, you know, but that it's a, it's like a, it's a totally different definition of what success is, but it doesn't disregard the rest of the world.

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:37.750
 Yeah.

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:38.390
 Yeah, exactly.

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:38.770
 Yeah.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:41.370
 And that's, we have this one foot in and one foot out.

00:29:42.230 --> 00:29:46.890
 And that's kind of, you know, a nice, a nice line to, it's a tricky line to walk.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:51.190
 Cause like we have to, you know, to have the parts that keep that one foot in the other part.

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:52.230
 Cause we just have to.

00:29:52.270 --> 00:30:00.390
 Well, and at the same time as we're trying to show our interns what a beautiful life it is, we're also trying to show them you can make a living doing this.

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:02.410
 We may not be doing the best job yet.

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:04.290
 We're doing, I mean, we are, it's just, it's hard.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:07.380
 The bottom line is really hard.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:08.640
 Costs are high.

00:30:08.780 --> 00:30:13.340
 So we try to share all that with them, but still giving them the sense of how worth it is.

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:16.640
 Well, no, but you can make it financially as well.

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:22.310
 Cause some farmers, I think it may be a little bit too like, Oh, well we get to eat well at least.

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:24.810
 And, you know, we have to be outside.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:26.390
 Like, well, you can eat well out of your garden.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:27.630
 Yeah, exactly.

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:29.430
 Like you can make a living as well.

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:32.250
 So it is important for us that they, they get that message.

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:32.710
 Yeah.

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:39.690
 Well, and I think we talked about the, when we saw you in August, August is like fame.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:44.230
 Farmers are famously starting to get extremely exhausted in August, right?

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:45.590
 You're like eating that watermelon.

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:48.170
 Everything's really beautiful, but you're like so tired.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:48.590
 Right.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:49.190
 Exactly.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:52.310
 That you can barely see straight and all the rest of those things.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:58.670
 Um, for me, when I hear the word farm, I think of it as a place, a working place.

00:30:58.850 --> 00:31:04.280
 I don't think of it as this pastoral passive place in the country.

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:09.080
 I think it needs working people that actually engage with it and work is work.

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:11.210
 Like some days it's really hard.

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:11.770
 Right.

00:31:11.830 --> 00:31:17.190
 So what does that like to be, to live on this Island that people see as this really dreamy

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:23.250
 place and do this work that people see as this really dreamy lifestyle and have those

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:28.410
 parts, but also, yeah, I think it just, it just makes us laugh.

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:31.090
 We respond to this a little bit differently, I think.

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:35.760
 Because I get, um, it is, it's so, it's everything you said.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:36.790
 It is beautiful.

00:31:36.790 --> 00:31:40.150
 And we love the community feel and I love the work.

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:42.850
 I actually miss working because I've been with Larkin so much the last year.

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:44.870
 So I'm a bit more dreamy about work than you are.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:46.270
 I've been working with an injury.

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:46.650
 Yeah.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:47.710
 For several months.

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:48.250
 Yeah.

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:54.360
 I find it, I guess I found it cute for a while, you know, but I find it super irritating.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:59.920
 Honestly, because every person that I talk to at the farmer's market or whatever they're

00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:02.160
 like, and you know, I know a million farmers can relate to the story.

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:05.320
 They're just like, you must just live in the dream.

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:09.700
 You know, it's really what I want with like my ultimate dream is to be doing what you're

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:10.220
 doing.

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:13.440
 Um, I'm hoping to move that direction in the next few years.

00:32:13.480 --> 00:32:14.360
 People all ages tell us that.

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:17.000
 I want to just really produce food for myself and my family.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:18.780
 And I'm like, well, that's not really what I'm doing.

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:19.240
 You know?

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.640
 And then it's, you try, you just like are nice.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:22.360
 You're like, I get yours.

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.300
 The sentiment is very sweet and I am very, I want, I'm doing exactly what I want to be

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:26.780
 doing too.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:27.060
 Yeah.

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:28.980
 And I, you know, you are living the dream.

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:29.880
 That's true.

00:32:30.460 --> 00:32:37.190
 But also you're like, I could just launch into a tirade about pigs getting out and fall,

00:32:37.310 --> 00:32:40.170
 you know, or whatever, whatever complication of the day is.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:42.950
 And like, you know, trying to be nice about it.

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:43.330
 Yeah.

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:43.610
 Yeah.

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:43.770
 Yeah.

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:44.130
 That's great.

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:45.730
 Yeah.

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:46.950
 We get that coming at the market.

00:32:47.110 --> 00:32:51.850
 Usually when, as any farmer knows, you've gone through a crazy harvest day the day before

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:54.950
 and you've been up at five and the van broke down on the way.

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:57.410
 You're over caffeinated to make up for it or whatever.

00:32:57.630 --> 00:33:00.910
 And you had to like lug all your heavy stuff and your tent's rusty and broken because you're

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:03.010
 trying to improve your bottom line, but not buy a new tent.

00:33:03.250 --> 00:33:03.530
 Yeah.

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:06.630
 And then someone shows up and it's like, wow, you guys are slim to you.

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:07.710
 Those moments are really funny.

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:09.690
 You're like, did you, when did you get out of bed this morning?

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:15.490
 But, but as Zach said, at the same time, we'll, I often stop and I'm like, well, yeah.

00:33:15.490 --> 00:33:15.710
 Yeah.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:16.130
 Yeah.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:16.450
 Totally.

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:21.090
 Because we work together outside and, you know, always have flowers on our counter and eat really

00:33:21.090 --> 00:33:23.250
 well and, you know, get to travel in the winter.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:27.030
 And like a lot of things are very from the outside, like living the dream.

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:32.230
 But then I have a friend that I recently met that's a skydiving instructor and she gets

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:33.490
 the same thing.

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:34.450
 Oh man.

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:37.210
 And I, we were talking about that the other night and she was like, you know, but some

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:41.370
 days I wake up and my body is sore and I'm like, I have to go jump out of a plane at 16,000

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:42.950
 feet five times or whatever.

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:44.910
 Like, yeah, that's crazy.

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:49.170
 And I think I fell into the, oh man, you know, I guess in an alternate life, that would be

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:50.590
 a pretty good, she's just, yeah.

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:51.830
 You know, we'd probably do that to people ourselves.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:52.170
 I'm sure.

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:52.990
 I'm sure we do.

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:53.650
 I don't even notice.

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:55.750
 Oh yeah.

00:33:55.810 --> 00:33:57.930
 I used to always say that to outdoor wilderness instructors.

00:33:58.030 --> 00:33:58.610
 Yeah, totally.

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:01.010
 I'd like to apologize to any artists.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:02.250
 Artists.

00:34:02.390 --> 00:34:03.090
 I'm sure artists.

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:03.450
 I'm sure.

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:03.770
 Yeah.

00:34:04.780 --> 00:34:08.370
 Well, maybe let's finish up hearing, what is this for Larkin?

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:10.360
 Larkin's their one-year-old daughter.

00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:15.870
 So she's the new, the new help on the farm amongst many other things.

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:19.270
 And she'll pick all the white tipped strawberries so well, I'm sure.

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:20.740
 Oh yeah, I'm sure.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:24.290
 But why, why raise a kid on a farm?

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:32.450
 Um, I think, okay, this is a big question.

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:34.190
 So they get tough?

00:34:34.490 --> 00:34:34.610
 No.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:35.410
 Okay, I'll handle this.

00:34:35.530 --> 00:34:35.990
 We're going to be able to.

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:36.390
 Because our farm's so dangerous.

00:34:36.490 --> 00:34:39.610
 That's just a joke about all the like boards and stuff that we haven't dealt with for a

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:39.950
 one-year-old.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:41.520
 She'll be fine.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:41.920
 She'll be fine.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:42.220
 She's tough.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:48.040
 I want her to learn the value of, of work that you feel good about.

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:50.260
 Um, and the value of play.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:52.480
 And our farm has a lot of play as a part of it.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:52.960
 It does.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:54.360
 We, we build that in a lot.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:55.600
 And also the value of time.

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:59.730
 Um, because like part core to our belief, even though I joke,

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:06.240
 about 80 hour weeks and that kind of stuff is that farmers deserve, I'm also like deserve

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:06.760
 time off.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:10.000
 And we take a pretty generous chunk of it in the winter and our systems built around that.

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:14.810
 And both of us, I know, really, really appreciate that time.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:18.610
 And maybe even more, it's maybe even more delicious because we have so little of it during

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:19.670
 other parts of the year.

00:35:20.330 --> 00:35:26.690
 Um, and I, yeah, I just, I want her to appreciate all of those things like that life is really

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:29.530
 enjoyable to live and, and very engaging.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:34.610
 Like I was saying, I love that farms have all these different ways to plug in and whatever

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:36.670
 you're good at, there's a place that you can help.

00:35:36.670 --> 00:35:37.610
 That boredom is not a thing.

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:37.870
 Yeah.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:41.520
 You know, there is no, that's just doesn't exist.

00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:42.790
 Yeah.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:44.170
 That's true.

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:48.680
 And yeah, I want her, there's all the, like the little parts of it.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:57.190
 I want her to learn to repair things and try, you know, experiment in the field and forage

00:35:57.190 --> 00:36:01.680
 and all the different things you can do on a, on a farm like ours.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:02.220
 Yeah.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:07.940
 She, you know, I want, especially as a daughter and I want her to, you know, be empowered to,

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:12.850
 you know, go, go out to the shop and get a hammer and hit whatever needs, hit the door

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:13.830
 jam back into square.

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:18.990
 Like I want her to see that we're constantly working on our life and that that's, you know,

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:22.820
 an important lesson to take is that you're, there's little pieces to your life that you're

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:23.580
 always working on.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:27.310
 And I want her to have all the skills to do that.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:33.250
 And whether that means whatever the hammer or the, you know, strawberry or the website

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:36.410
 design, like I want her to see how much goes into this.

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:41.710
 And also I want her to eat a lot of strawberries, you know, we get, I want, I want to see like

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:47.470
 little happy people engaged with this process, um, and knowing where their food comes from

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:48.850
 and being a part of it.

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:51.590
 And, you know, being the irritating kid that goes to their friend's house and I was like,

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:52.890
 yeah, tomatoes are out of season.

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:57.230
 You know, I want her to know what real quality in life is.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:03.410
 And I think farms, you know, provide that if nothing else, like quality of life is huge.

00:37:04.070 --> 00:37:06.230
 Well, thanks guys.

00:37:06.230 --> 00:37:08.130
 It's been so nice talking to you today.

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.180
 It's been so nice talking to you guys.

